    "q-29840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q29840",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "What is the antiderivative of $(\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the sum and constant factor rules and take the coefficients out of the integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4/3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{10}{x}-\\sqrt[3]{x})\\text{d}x & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =10(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|)-\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
