    "q-49101": {
      "dom_id": "step-q49101",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "What is the antiderivative of ${(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\frac{4}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1-\\frac{2}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{4}{x}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-49101-a",
